<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermann Göring</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hermann Goering</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermann Göring was a leading Nazi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> fighter pilot who became commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> and Hitler's designated successor. As minister-president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1933 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> and used the Prussian police against the regime's opponents. Convicted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> of war crimes and crimes against humanity, he killed himself in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-goring.jpg" alt="Hermann Göring" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Alexander, Office of the United States Chief of Counsel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Goering_-_Nuremberg_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hermann Wilhelm Göring was born on 12 January 1893 in Rosenheim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His father, Heinrich Ernst Göring, had been the first governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>. His godfather, Hermann Epenstein, a wealthy Jewish physician and businessman, provided the family with a home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and then with a small castle. In the First World War Göring served in an infantry regiment, then transferred to the air service. He became a fighter pilot, received the Pour le Mérite and was the last commander of Jagdgeschwader 1, the wing once led by Manfred von Richthofen. After the war he worked as a pilot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and married Carin von Kantzow on 3 February 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Göring joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1922 after hearing a speech by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and took command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmabteilung">Sturmabteilung</a> in March 1923. He was wounded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> that year and became addicted to morphine. When Hitler became chancellor on 30 January 1933, Göring was made minister without portfolio and, on 11 April, Minister-President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and chief of the Prussian police. He oversaw the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a>, which he ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> in 1934. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire of 27 February 1933 he called for a crackdown on Communists, and some 4,000 party members were arrested. In 1936 he became Plenipotentiary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-year-plan">Four Year Plan</a>.</p><p>On 1 September 1939 Hitler designated him his successor. After the Fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940 he received the specially created rank of Reichsmarschall. His standing declined as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> failed to stop Allied bombing and to supply the forces encircled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>, and he devoted his attention to collecting property and art, much of it stolen from Jewish victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. When he asked on 22 April 1945 to take over leadership of the Reich, Hitler regarded this as treason and ordered his arrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Göring supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> of 1935 and required the registration of all Jewish property. After Kristallnacht he proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> be fined one billion marks, and minutes read out at his trial showed his knowledge of and complicity in their persecution. He was convicted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-trials">Nuremberg trials</a> in 1946 of conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity. His request to be shot was refused and he was sentenced to hang. On the night of 15 October 1946, before the execution, he died by suicide by taking cyanide.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hermann Göring” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gestapo grew out of the Prussian political police, which came under <b>Hermann Göring</b> after Hitler named him Reich commissioner of the Interior for Prussia in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1936 <b>Hermann Göring</b>’s Four Year Plan pursued self-sufficiency in fuel, rubber and steel, while consumption was held down.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rosenheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Nuremberg Court Prison, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.86,12.13&amp;year=1927" aria-label="Rosenheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4833 488 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hermann Göring"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5283 758h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5283 758h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.86,12.13&amp;year=1927">What was here in 1927? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
